\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

Large Language Model (LLM) evaluation increasingly relies on LLM-as-a-judge protocols, where one or more LLM judges score model responses on structured benchmarks \citep{zheng2023mtbench}. Two well-documented sources of distortion affect these evaluations: \emph{judge bias}, where different judges assign systematically different scores to the same response \citep{wang2024positionbias, ye2024justice, panickssery2024selffavor}, and \emph{benchmark contamination}, where models achieve inflated scores on items encountered during training \citep{dcr2025, mink2024}. Existing methods address these distortions independently: judge bias corrections calibrate inter-judge differences without modeling contamination \citep{cyclicjudge2026, uda2026}, while contamination detection tools adjust for memorization without accounting for judge effects \citep{constat2024, dcr2025, logprober2024}. A practitioner who needs both corrections must apply them sequentially, but this produces unreliable estimates. Accurate ability ranking is essential for model selection, safety evaluation, and benchmark design; systematic distortion undermines all three.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig1_framework_v2}
\caption{(a)~Sequential pipelines address judge bias and contamination independently; omitted variable bias confounds the unmodeled facet into ability estimates. (b)~G-MFRM three-stage joint calibration: Stage~1 fits a standard MFRM for initial estimates $(\hat{\theta}, \hat{b}, \hat{d})$; Stage~2 estimates per-item exposure $e_i$ from external evidence (reference model anchoring, cross-model agreement, and optionally LogProber); Stage~3 performs joint MCMC inference with $e_i$ fixed, recovering contamination-corrected ability $\tilde{\theta}_n$, contamination susceptibility $c_n$, and judge severity $d_j$.}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure*}

We formalize this failure as an omitted variable bias (OVB) problem \citep{angrist2009mostly}. Within the Many-Facet Rasch Model (MFRM) framework \citep{linacre1989mfrm}, the complete data-generating process includes both judge severity $d_j$ and a contamination interaction $c_n \cdot e_i$. Fitting a model that omits either facet produces biased parameter estimates: a bias-first pipeline absorbs contamination into ability estimates, while a contamination-first pipeline misattributes judge severity as contamination signal. The bias is directional---contaminated models are systematically overestimated---and persists regardless of the ordering (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}).

We propose the Gated Many-Facet Rasch Model (G-MFRM), which jointly estimates model ability, judge severity, and contamination susceptibility in a single measurement framework. G-MFRM extends MFRM with a multiplicative gating term $c_n \cdot e_i$, inspired by Doubly-Gated IRT \citep{shu2013dgirt}, and employs a three-stage calibration procedure with externally anchored per-item exposure indicators (Figure~\ref{fig:framework}). We provide formal identifiability guarantees (Theorem~\ref{thm:sufficient}) and error propagation bounds for the staged estimation. In synthetic experiments ($N = 50$), G-MFRM reduces ability root mean squared error (RMSE) by 51.2\% relative to sequential estimation ($p < 0.001$). On Arena-Hard with 9 judges and 8 target models, G-MFRM yields nominally higher rank agreement with human preferences ($\tau = 0.857$ vs.\ $0.786$; not significant at $N = 8$) and substantially improved predictive fit ($\Delta\mathrm{ELPD} = -55.7$, $|\Delta/\mathrm{SE}| = 5.9$). Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The G-MFRM framework with formal identifiability guarantees and quantified error propagation bounds for staged estimation (Section~\ref{sec:method}).
    \item Synthetic validation confirming 51.2\% RMSE reduction over sequential estimation ($p < 0.001$; Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
    \item On Arena-Hard, the first joint diagnostic of per-model contamination susceptibility and per-judge severity within a measurement-theoretic framework (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Judge Bias in LLM Evaluation.}
LLM-as-a-judge evaluations are subject to multiple systematic biases. \citet{wang2024positionbias} demonstrate that LLM judges exhibit strong position bias in pairwise evaluation, where swapping the order of candidate responses can flip the ranking; \citet{li2024splitmerge} propose a split-and-merge strategy to align position biases across evaluators. Beyond positional effects, \citet{koo2024cobbler} benchmark six cognitive biases in 15 LLM evaluators, finding significant bias in approximately 40\% of comparisons, and \citet{ye2024justice} quantify verbosity and self-enhancement biases. \citet{panickssery2024selffavor} show that LLMs systematically favor their own generations, a confound that affects any evaluation where a judge model shares a family with a target. On the correction side, CyclicJudge \citep{cyclicjudge2026} eliminates average bias via round-robin assignment but treats residual variation as genuine ability. Unsupervised Debiasing Alignment (UDA) \citep{uda2026} learns a neural K-factor reducing inter-judge deviation by 63.4\% without modeling contamination. \citet{fiedler2026bias_uncertainty} formalize judge variability via $J/\Delta J$ diagnostics. \citet{bavaresco2025judgebench} evaluate 11 LLMs as judges across 20 NLP tasks, finding large variance across datasets and emphasizing the need for task-specific validation. All of these methods model only the judge facet, absorbing contamination effects into ability estimates (Section~\ref{sec:confounding}).

\paragraph{Benchmark Contamination Detection and Avoidance.}
Benchmark contamination arises when evaluation items appear in training data, inflating performance estimates. Detection methods take multiple approaches: ConStat \citep{constat2024} uses performance-based statistical tests comparing original and rephrased items; Data Contamination Risk (DCR) \citep{dcr2025} quantifies contamination via a unified metric integrating multiple evidence sources; LogProber \citep{logprober2024} leverages token-level log-probabilities to disentangle memorization from genuine capability; and Min-K\% \citep{mink2024} detects pretraining data membership through outlier token probabilities. \citet{golchin2024timetravel} propose guided instruction prompting to detect instance-level contamination, while \citet{oren2024proving} provide provable statistical guarantees for black-box contamination detection. \citet{deng2024contamination} and \citet{sainz2023contamination} investigate the prevalence and measurement of contamination across modern benchmarks. On the judge side, \citet{preference_leakage2026} identify preference leakage, where judge-side familiarity with benchmark items inflates scores---a complementary phenomenon to G-MFRM's examinee-side focus. Contamination avoidance strategies include dynamic benchmarks such as LiveBench \citep{livebench2025}, private evaluation via TRUCE \citep{truce2024}, and efficient subset selection via tinyBenchmarks \citep{polo2024tinybenchmarks}. G-MFRM takes a complementary approach: rather than preventing contamination, it corrects for residual contamination in existing benchmarks while simultaneously modeling judge effects.

\paragraph{Item Response Theory (IRT) and MFRM for LLM Evaluation.}
Item Response Theory provides a principled measurement framework for jointly modeling item difficulty and examinee ability. \citet{lalor2019irt} demonstrate that IRT item parameters can be learned from model ensembles (``artificial crowds'') without human responses. \citet{rodriguez2021irt} apply IRT to NLP leaderboards, showing that IRT-based rankings can identify annotation errors and highlight informative evaluation items. \citet{vania2021irt} use IRT to compare 29 NLP datasets across 18 models, revealing which benchmarks are saturated versus discriminative. In the multi-rater setting, the Many-Facet Rasch Model (MFRM) \citep{linacre1989mfrm, linacre1994mfrm} extends IRT to separate rater effects from examinee ability. \citet{jiao2025mfrm_llm} apply MFRM to compare ten LLM raters with human experts, demonstrating that MFRM separates genuine ability from systematic judge effects. \citet{uto2020generalized} propose a generalized MFRM with Bayesian estimation; \citet{choi2026irt_judge} diagnose LLM judge reliability via IRT. Pseudo-Siamese Network for IRT (PSN-IRT) \citep{zhou2025psnirt} extends IRT with neural architectures but does not include contamination facets. The Doubly-Gated IRT (DG-IRT) model of \citet{shu2013dgirt} is the closest antecedent, introducing binary gating for item preknowledge in educational testing; G-MFRM extends this to continuous exposure, multi-rater designs, and provides formal identifiability analysis not available for DG-IRT (Section~\ref{sec:model_spec}).

